      "writtenOn": "2017-05-02T13:01:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we can just always return false here if filp is NULL. The current code doesn\u0027t do that, it returns ((info-\u003ematch ^ info-\u003einvert) \u0026 (XT_QTAGUID_UID | XT_QTAGUID_GID)).\n\nI think what that means is:\n\n1. If the user specified --uid-owner or --gid-owner (or both), return false.\n2. If the user specified  no --uid-owner or --gid owner, or if all the UID/GID\n   matches are inverted (e.g., just ! --uid-owner, or just ! --gid-owner, or\n   both ! --uid-owner and ! --gid-owner), return true.\n\nI think #2 is so that if you configure a rule to \"allow all packets that are not UID 1000\", then a packet on a closed socket (that doesn\u0027t have a UID) is allowed. That\u0027s because the socket doesn\u0027t have UID 1000 - it doesn\u0027t have any UID.\n\nWith that in mind: if I\u0027m reading the code correctly, if we get here then sk is not null, and if there was a UID match, then that match accepted the packet. So all we need to do is check whether a GID match was specified. More specifically:\n\n- If there\u0027s a GID match, return false.\n- If there\u0027s an inverted GID match, return true.\n\nI think that means:\n\n\tres \u003d ((info-\u003ematch ^ info-\u003einvert) \u0026 XT_QTAGUID_GID) \u003d\u003d 0;\n\nDoes that sound correct?",
      "parentUuid": "bb539e76_19714567",
